{"role":"assistant","content":"Done. Here's the summary:\n\n## Cause\n\n`similarity()` in `src/app.py:24` returned a Python `float` (`round(0.7*tag_score + 0.3*word_score, 4)`), which landed in each new item's `similar` list. DynamoDB has no float type — boto3's serializer only accepts `Decimal` for numbers — so `put_item` raised `TypeError: Float types are not supported`, caught by the broad `except` in `handler()` and returned as 500.\n\nIt only shows up on the *second* publish because the first one stores `similar: []` (empty table → no floats → stores fine). From the second publish on, `similar` always contains a float `score`, so every publish fails. I reproduced this exactly before fixing: first publish 201, second 500, same traceback as `logs/error.log`.\n\n## Fix\n\n- `src/app.py` — `similarity()` now does all its math in `Decimal` (ratios as `Decimal/Decimal`, weights as `Decimal(\"0.7\")`/`Decimal(\"0.3\")`), so the score is DynamoDB-safe by construction, not converted at the boundary.\n- `tests/test_publish.py` — added `test_second_publish_stores_similar_as_dynamodb_safe_numbers`: publishes two overlapping stories through `handler()`, asserts both return 201 and that stored scores are `Decimal`. Since `FakeTable.put_item` validates items exactly like boto3's serializer, any float reaching `put_item` from any code path will now fail the test.\n\n## Verification\n\n`python3 -m unittest discover tests` — 2 tests pass. Replayed the logged scenario end-to-end: three consecutive publishes all return 201 with `Decimal` scores. Full cause and fix are in `ANSWER.md`."}
